A||N||6||RiceBurner515||Performance O2 sensor||||5||
Z||000000||RiceBurner515||11-16-2000||11:34 PM||||does puting a brand new O2 sensor on increase performance? Not like racing and speed wise but I mean such as making the car better running? Is a new one better than an old one or does it just not matter, as long as it works?||||reg||5
Z||000001||maninblack||11-17-2000||12:45 AM||||It depends how old , your old one is,but I would put a new one just incase,it's not expensive and anyone can do it in their own garage.other than that if your car is fine<BR>you can leave it for now,an o2 sensor is just for calculating your fuel mixture,so a new one might have a more correct reading,thus increasing your performance.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-17-2000||09:10 AM||||Rated life of O2 sensor is 15-20,000 miles. If you don't know how old it is I'd change it. A faky O2 sensor can cause a number of weird problmes and often won't set a code. Replace the TPS while your at it. That one has mechanical parts that wear or get dirty.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||11-17-2000||07:56 PM||||I usually change out the O2 sensor the day of a smog check. Ive passed every time with ridiculously low numbers!<P>But as far as raised performance?<P>Nope.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||11-17-2000||09:31 PM||||Yes it increases performance because it gives your computer the correct air fuel readings. When the readings are off either to rich or to lean your engines performance suffers. More importantly though an O2 sensor effects you’re gas mileage more than anything.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cheever2||11-17-2000||10:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maninblack:<BR><B>... anyone can do it in their own garage...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> When I replaced mine, the old connector broke, so I just cut it off and went wire-to-wire without it.  Later, when in the shop, they said it needed a sensor (among other things) so I let them replace it, connector and all.  Is that connector important? <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-18-2000||01:05 PM||||The main reason you wnat the connector is that it's critical the sensor have a solid connection. The shop didn't feel comfortable with the elimination of it and rightfully so. They don't want to be blamed later if there's a problem because of the missing connector.<P>Keep in mind the O2 sensor is the only one that generates it's own signal voltage. It's a very low 1 volt, less most of the time. If there is any problem with the O2 sensor wire or it's gound return wire, which is bolted to the block, then the O2 sensor will not work right.<P>Also the O2 sensor has a rated life of 15-20,000 miles. You don't want to have to repeatedly splice the wires each time it's changed. You'll eventually run out of wire and have to add more splices to make it reach.||||reg||1
